Break & Continue
- "continue" brings control to the beginning of the loop; does not go through the code behind "continue and skips to the condition of the loop
   In FOR statements, if the code reaches to "continue," it executes the update expression (i.g. i++, i--), and then evaluates the condition.
- "continue" can only be used inside loops: for, while, and do-while (iteration statements)

For-Each Loop
- For-Each loop is a way to go through every item in an array or collection, one at a time, without managing an index yourself.
- For-Each loop stops when there are no more elements left to take. You don't have to write down the condition unlike For loop, but Java handles it.
- A for-each loop has no built-in access to the index. However, you can keep
    track of it manually by declaring a counter variable BEFORE the loop and
    incrementing it (e.g., j++) inside the loop body.
    Ex) int j = 0;
        for (String fruit : fruits) {
            System.out.println(j + "\t" + fruit);
            j++;
        }


